I returned from the graveyard, I was searching for your horse in the ravines,
Instead of the horse, wretched me, I stumbled right into the devis.
One met me, clad in a black felt cloak, of an enormous build:
Still before my eyes flashes the foulness of his skin;
Big-eared, teeth-gnashing, hideous, black in color;
He reached out his hands to me, hands as large as a head,
A blackish leather hat on his head appeared the size of a mountain.
He told me: "Come with me, live with me, woman,
I have much gold and silver, I shall hide nothing from you."
I fled, I was terrified, the devi pursued me with a roar;
The whole earth shook with a rumble beneath his feet;
This sound chilled me, as mountains and plains spun to and fro,
I barely reached here to tell you of my woe with a rasping breath.

